#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:58
msgid "Model Equations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:60
msgid ""
"Here we briefly outline the models used for the open-circuit potential, "
"kinetics, and solid phase transport used in the MSMR model, as described in "
"Baker and Verbrugge (2018). The remaining physics is modelled differently "
"depending on which options are selected. By default, the rest of the battery "
"model is as described in Maquis et al. (2019). In the following we give "
"equations for a single electrode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:72
msgid "Thermodynamics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:74
msgid ""
"The MSMR model is developed by assuming that all electrochemical reactions "
"at the electrode/electrolyte interface in a lithium insertion cell can be "
"expressed in the form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:76
msgid ""
"\\text{Li}^{+} + \\text{e}^{-} + \\text{H}_{j} \\rightleftharpoons  "
"(\\text{Li--H})_{j}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:78
msgid ""
"For each species :math:`j`, a vacant host site :math:`\\text{H}_{j}` can "
"accommodate one lithium leading to a filled host site :math:`(\\text{Li--"
"H})_{j}`. The OCV for this reaction is written as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:80
msgid ""
"U_j = U_j^0 + \\frac{\\omega_j}{f}\\log\\left(\\frac{X_j - x_j}"
"{x_j}\\right),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:82
msgid ""
"where :math:`f = F/(RT)`, and :math:`R`, :math:`T`, and :math:`F` are the "
"universal gas constant, temperature in Kelvin, and Faraday’s constant, "
"respectively. Here :math:`X_j` represents the total fraction of available "
"host sites which can be occupied by species :math:`j`, :math:`x_j` is the "
"fraction of filled sites occupied by species :math:`j`, :math:`U_j^0` is a "
"concentration independent standard electrode potential, and the :math:"
"`\\omega_j` is an unitless parameter that describes the level of disorder of "
"the reaction represented by gallery :math:`j`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:85
msgid "The equation for each reaction can be inverted to give"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:87
msgid ""
"x_j = \\frac{X_j}{1+\\exp[f(U-U_j^0)/\\omega_j]}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:89
msgid ""
"The overall electrode state of charge is given by summing the fractional "
"occupancies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:91
msgid ""
"x = \\sum_j x_j = \\sum_j \\frac{X_j}{1+\\exp[f(U-U_j^0)/\\omega_j]},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:93
msgid ""
"which is an explicit closed form expression for the inverse of the OCV. This "
"is opposite to many battery models where one typically gives the OCV as an "
"explicit function of the state of charge (or stoichiometry)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:95
msgid ""
"At a particle interface with the electrolyte, local equilibrium requires that"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:97
msgid ""
"U_j = U(x) \\quad \\forall j.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:109
msgid "Kinetics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:111
msgid "The kinetics of the insertion reaction are given as"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:113
msgid ""
"i_j = i_{0,j}[e^{(1-\\alpha_j)f\\eta} - e^{-\\alpha_jf\\eta}], \\qquad i = "
"\\sum_j i_j,\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:115
msgid ""
"where :math:`i_j` is the interfacial current associated with reaction :math:"
"`j`, :math:`\\alpha_j` is the symmetry factor, :math:`\\eta` is the "
"overpotential, given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:117
msgid ""
"\\eta = \\phi_s - \\phi_e - U(x),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:119
msgid ""
"where :math:`\\phi_s` and :math:`\\phi_e` are the solid phase and "
"electrolyte potentials, respectively, and :math:`i_{0,j}` is the exchange "
"current density of reaction :math:`j`, given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:121
msgid ""
"i_{0,j} = i_{0,j}^{ref}(x_j)^{\\omega_j\\alpha_j}(X_j-x_j)^{\\omega_j(1-"
"\\alpha_j)}(c_e/c_e^{ref})^{1-\\alpha_j},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:123
msgid "where :math:`c_e` is the electrolyte concentration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:135
msgid "Solid phase transport"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:137
msgid ""
"Within the MSMR framework, the flux within the particles is expressed in "
"terms of gradient of the chemical potential"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:139
msgid ""
"N = -c_{\\text{T}}x\\frac{D}{RT}\\nabla \\mu + x(N+N_{\\text{H}}),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:141
msgid ""
"where :math:`N` is the flux of lithiated sites, :math:`N_{\\text{H}}` is the "
"flux of unlithiated sites, :math:`c_{\\text{T}}` is the total concentration "
"of lithiated and delithiated sites, and :math:`D` is a diffusion "
"coefficient. Ignoring volumetric expansion during lithiation, the total flux "
"of sites vanishes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:143
msgid ""
"N+N_{\\text{H}}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:145
msgid "It can then be shown that"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:147
msgid ""
"N = c_{\\text{T}}fDx(1-x)\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}x}\\nabla x.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:149
msgid "A mass balance in the solid phase then gives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:151
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} = -\\nabla\\cdot\\left(x(1-"
"x)fD\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}x}\\nabla x\\right),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:153
msgid ""
"which, for a radially symmetric spherical particle, must be solved subject "
"to the boundary conditions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:155
msgid ""
"N\\big\\vert_{r=0} = 0, \\quad N\\big\\vert_{r=R} = \\frac{i}{F},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:157
msgid ""
"where :math:`R` is the particle radius. This must be supplemented with a "
"suitable initial condition for the electrode state of charge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:159
msgid ""
"Solution of this problem requires evaluate of the function :math:`U(x)` and "
"the derivative :math:`\\text{d}U/\\text{d}x`, but these functions cannot be "
"explicitly integrated. This problem can be avoided by replacing the "
"dependent variable :math:`x` with a new dependent variable :math:`U` subject "
"to the transformation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:161
msgid ""
"x = \\sum_j \\frac{X_j}{1+\\exp[f(U-U_j^0)/\\omega_j]}.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:163
msgid "This gives the following equation for mass balance within the particles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:165
msgid ""
"\\frac{\\text{d}U}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial t} = -"
"\\nabla\\cdot\\left(x(1-x)fD\\nabla x\\right),\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:167
msgid ""
"which must be solved along with the transformed boundary and initial "
"conditions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:179
msgid "Parameterization of the MSMR model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:181
msgid "The behaviour of MSMR model is characterised by the parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:183
msgid ":math:`X_j`: ``host site occupancy fraction (j)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:184
msgid ":math:`U^0_j`: ``host site standard potential (j) [V]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:185
msgid ":math:`\\omega_j`: ``host site ideality factor (j)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:186
msgid ":math:`\\alpha_j`: ``host site charge transfer coefficient (j)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:187
msgid ""
":math:`i_{0,j}^{ref}`: ``host site reference exchange-current density (j) [A."
"m-2]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:189
msgid ""
"Let's take a look at their values in the example parameter set provided in "
"PyBaMM. The thermodynamic parameter values are taken from Verbrugge et al. "
"(2017) and correspond to a graphite negative electrode and NMC positive "
"electrode. The remaining value are based on a parameterization of the LG M50 "
"cell, from Chen et al. (2020)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:191
msgid ""
"We first load in the MSMR model and specify the number of reactions in each "
"electrode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.MSMR({\"number of MSMR reactions\": (\"6\", "
"\"4\")})"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:212
msgid "Then we can inspect the parameter values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/MSMR.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"parameter_values = model.default_parameter_values\n"
"\n"
"# Loop over domains\n"
"for domain in [\"negative\", \"positive\"]:\n"
"    Electrode = domain.capitalize()\n"
"    # Loop over reactions\n"
"    N = int(parameter_values[\"Number of reactions in \" + domain + \" "
"electrode\"])\n"
"    for i in range(N):\n"
"        names = [\n"
"            f\"{Electrode} electrode host site occupancy fraction ({i})\",\n"
"            f\"{Electrode} electrode host site standard potential ({i}) "
"[V]\",\n"
"            f\"{Electrode} electrode host site ideality factor ({i})\",\n"
"            f\"{Electrode} electrode host site charge transfer coefficient "
"({i})\",\n"
"            f\"{Electrode} electrode host site reference exchange-current "
"density ({i}) [A.m-2]\",\n"
"        ]\n"
"        for name in names:\n"
"            print(f\"{name} = {parameter_values[name]}\")"
msgstr ""

